  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 245 AM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nRain is forecast to end by Sunday morning with clouds mostly\nsticking around. High temperatures will be warm in the low to\nmid 70s for the day. We'll have a brief moment of mid-level high\npressure over us on Sunday, but it's quickly ushered out by an\nincoming bowling ball of a trough. With this incoming low\npressure system, we'll see gusty winds, a slight temperature\ndrop, and the possibility of thunderstorms. \n\nWe'll start to feel the impacts of the incoming trough on Monday\nas southeasterly winds become gusty near 30-35 mph by late\nmorning with the strongest gusts along and west of Highway 65.\nIf these winds trend any higher, we'll have to watch for a Wind\nAdvisory. Rain chances begin to increase overnight Monday into \nTuesday (50-90%) with a Marginal to Slight risk of severe \nweather possible mostly for areas along and west of I-49 with \nthe Slight being further west. Right now, the best severe risk \nappears to be over central KS, with the western CWA getting \nclipped. This is likely due to the base of the trough going \nthrough that area before the trough becomes elongated as it \nmoves over us due to another incoming mid- level trough over the\nGreat Lakes region. This northern system appears to impact our \nsevere weather chances, so we'll have to monitor the timing and \nplacement of these two systems to see if get more than 250 J/kg \nof CAPE. We still may see thunderstorms with this system, but \nthey may not be severe. However, the rainfall accumulations are \nforecast to be near 1 to 2 inches across the area which will be \nwelcomed!\n\nThe low pressure system that moves through at the beginning of\nthe week will drop our temperatures, but only by about 10\ndegrees. Though, it will bring them closer to average for this\ntime of year with highs in the 60s and lows in the 40s for the\nrest of the week.",
